For many buyers, the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; or &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max is the better choice. They offer the same A20 Pro chip as the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, a more versatile camera system, and a substantially lower price. Choose the Pro if you prefer a smaller, lighter phone, or pay &#36;100 more for the Pro Max if you want a larger screen and longer battery life.<br/>
<br/>
The camera system is the clearest reason to choose a Pro model. Its variable aperture adjusts automatically during everyday photography, while also giving photographers greater control over depth of field. The dedicated Telephoto camera provides much more flexibility for distant subjects, from performances and sporting events to travel photography. These advantages matter even if you never use professional capture formats.<br/>
<br/>
Buyers who rely on ProRAW, ProRes, Apple Log 2, or the Pro models' full manual-control suite have an even stronger reason to choose an &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;. While the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; does offer some manual camera controls, its selection of tools and formats is much more limited.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; is worth considering if you would regularly unfold it for reading, documents, viewing photos, watching video, or using two apps side by side. Think through a typical day and identify your specific tasks that would benefit from the inner display, such as keeping a reference document open while writing an email. &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; support could also make it useful for handwritten notes and annotations, but that support is coming later in 2026.<br/>
<br/>
Being able to prop the phone up for a call or video, give someone a live preview while photographing them, or use StandBy away from a charger may be useful throughout the day. Buyers who want to experience Apple's first foldable may also value the novelty and flexibility of the design, even without a specific productivity need.<br/>
<br/>
You still need to be comfortable with the changes to everyday features. &zwnj;Touch ID&zwnj; replaces &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj;, and there is no Action button for a customizable shortcut. The outer front camera has a lower resolution than the Pro models' camera, while the inner camera is designed around &zwnj;FaceTime&zwnj; and lacks several photography features. If selfies or front-camera video are important to you, those differences deserve consideration alongside the rear cameras.<br/>
<br/>
Try to assess the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; when it is closed as well as open. Despite its smaller outer display, it is wider and thicker than either Pro model and slightly heavier than the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max. Handling it in person in an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-store/">Apple Store</a> should help establish whether its width, folded thickness, and weight suit the way you normally carry and use your phone.<br/>
<br/>
Battery life also depends on how you use the two displays. Apple's U.S. figures put the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; level with the smaller Pro for typical use, below the Pro Max. Its long outer-display video playback rating does not carry over to the inner display, so buyers planning to spend much of the day unfolded should pay particular attention to those separate figures.<br/>
<br/>
The price gap remains significant at every storage tier. A 1TB &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max costs &#36;100 less than the entry-level &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, which has 256GB of storage. At the same capacity, the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; costs &#36;700 more than the Pro Max or &#36;800 more than the Pro. Buyers who need substantial local storage therefore have another reason to favor a Pro model.<br/>
<br/>
The premium can also extend to repairs and coverage. In the United States, Apple's estimated <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/14/iphone-duo-repair-fees/">inner-display repair fee</a> is &#36;999 without <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/applecare/">AppleCare</a>+, compared with &#36;329 for the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;'s display and &#36;379 for the Pro Max's. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/14/iphone-duo-cover-layer-and-battery-fees/">Battery service costs &#36;229</a>, compared with &#36;129 for either Pro model. A separate replacement of the inner display's cover layer is listed at &#36;49 without coverage or &#36;19 with &zwnj;AppleCare&zwnj;+.<br/>
<br/>
Standalone &zwnj;AppleCare&zwnj;+ with Theft and Loss also costs more for the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, at &#36;19.99 per month in the United States rather than &#36;14.99 for either Pro model. Buyers planning to insure their phone should include that recurring difference when comparing ownership costs.<br/>
<br/>
Choose the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; if its inner display would meaningfully improve the things you do on your phone, or if experiencing Apple's latest radical rethink of the iPhone is itself worth the premium and compromises to you. If you expect to spend most of your time on the outer display, the Pro models are easier to justify: You would otherwise be paying substantially more while making limited use of the feature that most distinguishes the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;.<br/>
